# Test: VPP backend REJECTS an out-of-range dscp filter value at verify time.
# VALIDATES: spec-followup-vpp-traffic AC-2/AC-6 boundary -- with dscp now
#            ACCEPTED as police-by-dscp (see traffic-vpp-accept-dscp-filter.ci), a
#            dscp VALUE above the 6-bit range (0-63) is still rejected at verify
#            with "out of range". The classify TOS/TC mask covers exactly the
#            6-bit DSCP field, so 64 has no representation.
# PREVENTS: regression where accepting dscp filters also accepts nonsensical
#           out-of-range values that would silently truncate on the wire.
#
# Replaced coverage note: this file previously asserted a blanket dscp
# REJECTION. Per the 2026-07-10 USER decision (police-by-dscp), dscp is now
# accepted; the acceptance is pinned by traffic-vpp-accept-dscp-filter.ci and the
# fakeOps unit tests, and this file now pins the value bound instead.
#
# Scope note: the daemon boot path this test drives does NOT reach the vpp
# Verifier. OnConfigure (internal/component/traffic/register.go) parses and
# applies; only OnConfigVerify calls RunVerifier, and that fires on a reload.
# The 64 bound is rejected earlier, by the shared config parser, which is what
# the assertion below observes. The vpp Verifier keeps its own bound check
# (verifyFilter), covered by the fakeOps unit tests.

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}

traffic {
	control {
		backend vpp
		interface eth0 {
			qdisc {
				type htb
				default-class default
				class control {
					rate 10mbit
					ceil 100mbit
					priority 0
					match dscp {
						value 64
					}
				}
				class default {
					rate 90mbit
					ceil 100mbit
					priority 1
					match protocol {
						value 6
					}
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

option=env:var=ze.log.traffic:value=info

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
# The await fence holds the daemon until its relayed stderr carries the needle,
# replacing the blind hold that guessed how long that took. The needle carries no
# ':' because parseAwait kv-splits the value, so it starts after any log prefix;
# the expect below still asserts the full phrase. The exit-code expectation is
# gone because awaitDaemonStderr owns the wait arm in runner_exec.go, so it could
# never fire. Every stderr assertion is unchanged.
# Producer, measured: internal/core/dscp/dscp.go Parse, "dscp value %d out of
# range (0-%d)", wrapped by parseFilterValue (internal/component/traffic/
# config.go) and surfaced by the traffic plugin OnConfigure as
# "traffic-control config: ... filter dscp: dscp value 64 out of range (0-63)".
await=stderr:contains=out of range
expect=stderr:contains=out of range
